.he "write (1)                    9/30/26                    write (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
write
- write to another user
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
write user [ ttyname ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Write
copies lines from your terminal to the terminal of another user.  A
line is sent when you press return, so the two of you may hold a
conversation; both terminals are dumb, and what one of you types appears
on the other's screen only if the other's terminal echoes what it is
sent.  End the conversation with an end of file, which sends the word
.bd
EOF
to the other end.
.sp
The other user is looked up in /etc/utmp and must be logged in.  If the
name appears there more than once - the same user logged in on two
terminals - you are told which one is being written to, and may name the
terminal yourself with the second argument to choose the other.
.sp
At the start the other terminal is sent
.sp
.in +5
.ti +5
Message from user tty...
.sp
.in -5
naming you and your terminal, and everything typed from then on
follows it.
.sp
A line of your input whose first character is an exclamation mark is not
sent as it stands.  What follows the mark is run by the shell as a
command, its output goes to your terminal and not to the other user's,
and an exclamation mark is printed on your terminal when the command
finishes.  It is meant for running something whose output you want to
send on, but the output is not caught and so has to be copied by hand.
.sp
Writing needs the other terminal to be writable by you.  A terminal that
is not, or one that cannot be opened within five seconds - a terminal
with no carrier, typically - is refused, and write says so and stops
rather than holding your terminal.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
There is no
.bd
write
page in the recovered 1982 manual, so none of the above is transcribed
from one; it is what the v7 source does and what this port keeps.
.sp
It is the terminal, not the user, that has the last word.  Write does
not ask the other user whether they want to be written to, and there is
no
.bd
mesg
here for them to answer with: if the terminal's mode lets the open
succeed, the message arrives.
.sp
Like who, write has to know which terminal it is on and there is no
ttyname in this tree's library, so it finds its own by reading the /dev
directory and asking the system about each name until one has the device
and the inode that the system reports for its terminal.
.sp
A user name beginning with a dash is not matched against the names in
/etc/utmp but taken as a request for the first terminal in the file.
That is the behavior of the original; it is not useful and is not
recommended.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/utmp	(current user information)
.br
/dev/*		the terminals written to
.br
/bin/sh		run for a ! line
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
